Realm device runner
===================

An XCUITest that Realm builds, signs with the user's own Apple Development identity, installs on
their iPhone and runs with `xcodebuild test-without-building`. It serves the phone's screen, its
element tree and touch, text and button input over HTTP on the phone's loopback interface, which
Realm reaches through usbmuxd.

This directory contains code derived from Maestro's iOS XCTest runner
(maestro-ios-xctest-runner/maestro-driver-iosUITests), Copyright mobile.dev, Inc., licensed under
the Apache License, Version 2.0 (see LICENSE), taken from
https://github.com/mobile-dev-inc/Maestro at commit 3fd3ea4ee979907e4c5f629603fc32bd55178b2b.

Derived: RealmDeviceRunner/Synthesis.swift, from Routes/XCTest/EventRecord.swift,
PointerEventPath.swift and RunnerDaemonProxy.swift — the private XCTest calls that synthesize a
touch, a drag and typed text at a point.

Changed by Realm, 2026:
  - FlyingFox (the Swift package Maestro's server is built on) is replaced by a small HTTP server on
    Network.framework (Server.swift), so building the runner fetches nothing from the network.
  - The routes are Realm's own, and fewer: status, device, hierarchy, screenshot, tap, swipe, text,
    key, button and open (Routes.swift, Screen.swift).
  - Maestro's automatic answering of system permission alerts (SystemPermissionHelper) and its
    setPermissions, launchApp, terminateApp, eraseText, keyboard and setOrientation routes are not
    carried over. The runner never answers an alert on its own.
  - The side button (lock) is not exposed: a phone the runner locked is one only its owner can
    unlock again.
  - EventRecord passes the interface orientation as an integer through the method's own signature,
    rather than as a boxed object through `perform(_:with:with:)`.
  - A double tap is one event record of two touches, so the gap between them is the device's and
    not an HTTP round trip's.
